\datethis
@* Introduction.
This program prints the primes below |limit|, using the sieve of
Eratosthenes. We state the overall shape of the program first and
defer every detail to a later section.

@d limit=50

@p program primes(output);
var @<Global variables@>@;
begin
  @<Cross out the composites@>;
  @<Print the survivors@>;
end.

@* The sieve. The array |composite| records what we have crossed out.
@<Global variables@>=
composite: array[2..limit] of boolean;
p,q,n: integer;

@ We need only cross out multiples of $p$ starting at $p^2$, because
every smaller multiple has a smaller factor and is already gone.
@<Cross out the composites@>=
for n:=2 to limit do composite[n]:=false;
p:=2;
while p*p<=limit do
  begin
  if not composite[p] then
    begin
    q:=p*p;
    while q<=limit do begin composite[q]:=true; q:=q+p; end;
    end;
  p:=p+1;
  end

@ Whatever survives is prime.
@<Print the survivors@>=
for n:=2 to limit do
  if not composite[n] then write(n:4);
writeln
